---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { MAGNITUDES } from "../lib/magnitudes";
---

<Layout
  title="How big is a million? — an interactive explainer"
  description="One dot is one. Multiply it by ten, seven times. The dot never changes size — only how far back you are standing."
>
  <a class="skip" href="#stage">Skip to the explainer</a>

  <header class="masthead wrap">
    <nav aria-label="Primary">
      <a href="./" class="masthead-home">How big is a million?</a>
      <a href="#notes">About these numbers</a>
    </nav>
  </header>

  <main class="wrap">
    <section class="intro">
      <h1>How big is a million?</h1>
      <p class="lede">Large numbers are easy to read and hard to feel.</p>
      <p class="sub">
        One dot is one. Multiply by ten, seven times. The dot never changes
        size — the only thing that moves is how far back you are standing.
      </p>
    </section>

    <div class="explainer">
      <figure class="stage" id="stage" data-core-output>
        <div class="stage-frame" data-frame>
          <canvas
            class="stage-canvas"
            data-canvas
            role="img"
            aria-label={MAGNITUDES[0].aria}></canvas>
          <noscript>
            {/* The canvas is supported but never painted when script is off,
                so it is a blank box with nothing to say for itself. The seven
                magnitudes below are all still there. */}
            <p class="stage-noscript">
              The drawing needs JavaScript. Every magnitude it would have drawn
              is written out below.
            </p>
          </noscript>
        </div>

        <figcaption class="stage-caption">
          {/*
            All seven magnitudes ship in the HTML. Script marks the page
            data-js="on", and only then does the CSS collapse the inactive ones
            — so without JavaScript this is a readable list of every magnitude
            rather than a blank screen. See CLAUDE.md: content visibility never
            depends on script.
          */}
        <ol class="magnitudes" aria-live="polite">
          {
            MAGNITUDES.map((magnitude) => (
              <li class="magnitude" data-magnitude={magnitude.step}>
                <p class="magnitude-figure">
                  <span class="magnitude-numeral">{magnitude.numeral}</span>
                  <span class="magnitude-name">{magnitude.name}</span>
                </p>
                <p class="magnitude-note">{magnitude.note}</p>
                <p class="magnitude-counting">
                  Counted aloud, one per second, without stopping:
                  <strong>{magnitude.counting}</strong>.
                </p>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ol>
        <p class="magnitude-measure" data-measure></p>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="controls">
      {/*
        The slider is first among the controls on purpose. It steps
        natively on the arrow keys from step 0, so the keyboard is served
        by a real control rather than by handlers of mine — and check:render,
        which focuses the first [data-core-interaction] and sends real key
        events, exercises that same path.
      */}
        <div class="controls-scale">
          <label class="controls-label" for="magnitude-scale">
            Magnitude
          </label>
          <input
            id="magnitude-scale"
            class="controls-slider"
            type="range"
            min="0"
            max="6"
            step="1"
            value="0"
            list="magnitude-ticks"
            aria-describedby="controls-hint"
            aria-valuetext="1, one"
            data-core-interaction
            data-scale
          />
          <datalist id="magnitude-ticks">
            {
              MAGNITUDES.map((magnitude) => (
                <option value={magnitude.step} label={magnitude.numeral} />
              ))
            }
          </datalist>
          <ul class="controls-marks" aria-hidden="true">
            <li>1</li>
            <li>10</li>
            <li>100</li>
            <li>1K</li>
            <li>10K</li>
            <li>100K</li>
            <li>1M</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="controls-steps">
          <button
            type="button"
            class="button"
            data-core-interaction
            data-back
            disabled
          >
            <span aria-hidden="true">÷10</span>
            <span><span class="sr-only">Ten times </span>fewer</span>
          </button>
          <button type="button" class="button" data-core-interaction data-next>
            <span aria-hidden="true">×10</span>
            <span><span class="sr-only">Ten times </span>more</span>
          </button>
        </div>

        <p class="controls-hint" id="controls-hint">
          Drag the scale, press the buttons, or use <kbd>←</kbd> and
          <kbd>→</kbd> anywhere on the page.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="payoff" data-payoff>
        Every dot on that screen is the one you started with, at the same size.
        You have already looked at all of them, one hundred at a time. You just
        cannot find the first one any more — and that is the whole difference
        between reading <em>a million</em> and meaning it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <section class="notes" id="notes">
      <h2>About these numbers</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          There is no dataset here. The only data is the powers of ten, and the
          drawing contains exactly as many dots as the number says.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Counting time</strong> is arithmetic on the magnitude: one
          number per second, no pauses and no sleep. A million seconds is
          eleven and a half days.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The size of one dot</strong> is measured from the drawing on
          your screen, at your window size, in CSS pixels. Resize the window and
          the figure changes, because it is a measurement rather than a claim.
        </li>
        <li>
          Past a certain zoom a single dot is smaller than one pixel, so it
          cannot be drawn as a dot at all. Where that happens each block is
          filled at exactly the fraction of its area the dots cover — the same
          average brightness they would have made, not a stand-in for a number
          the screen cannot hold. That the screen runs out before the number
          does is the point, so it would be a strange thing to fake.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </main>

  <footer class="wrap">
    <p>
      Built for COMP4020 at the ANU. One idea, one mechanic, and a dot that
      refuses to get bigger.
    </p>
  </footer>

  <script>
    import { start } from "../scripts/explainer";

    start();
  </script>
</Layout>
